  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "When you're installing a new window, getting the rough opening dimensions spot on is absolutely crucial. You can't just guess or go by a general idea of the window's size; you need to measure the actual window unit itself to ensure a perfect fit. The opening needs to be sized a bit larger than the window's frame to allow for shimming and adjustments during installation. Here’s a quick rundown of the process to get it right. First, you'll want to lay out the exact location of your new window opening on the wall. Then, carefully frame the sides and header, making sure everything is sturdy and secure. The really important part is verifying the rough opening at multiple points—don't just measure once! Check the width at the top, middle, and bottom, and the height on both sides. This helps account for any subtle bowing in your wall studs. You also need to confirm that the opening is perfectly plumb, level, and square. Accuracy here is key, because an incorrect rough opening can lead to delays when your window arrives or create problems with proper sealing and operation down the road. If you're feeling unsure about any of these steps, especially if it involves a load-bearing wall, give us a call at 303-505-0866. We can help make sure your window framing is done right the first time."
    }
  ],
